Okay, cool. So what I love about this, because the Tableau paper, I think the original Tableau paper came

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:08.859
out in like 2002. And at the time it was this research prototype called Polaris. And it was kind of funny too, when

00:25:08.859 --> 00:25:13.780
you read the paper, because the paper is, when you read the paper, the introduction is basically making the case

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:21.619
for, hey, this community, this is why we should care about what databases do and how we can map our visualization

00:25:21.619 --> 00:25:27.859
primitives onto database primitives to automatically generate SQL queries to process our data. That's basically

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:36.060
what that paper was bringing to the Viz community. It was saying, A, we should care about this. And B, our theory and

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:41.540
database theory could actually be integrated to produce this really nice automated stack going top to bottom and

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:48.540
bottom to top. And the grammar that kind of tied it all together is this, this grammar called VizQL. Basically this

00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:53.459
mapping across the stacks, because you can think of it as a stack, right? You have your data processing and storage

00:25:53.459 --> 00:25:59.739
layers, which the database management community typically owns. And then you have the visualization and

00:25:59.739 --> 00:26:05.780
interaction and user interface design layers, which the visualization community typically owns. And that paper was

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:14.339
saying, why, you know, bisect the problem in that way when you can actually do this more integrated approach. And

00:26:14.819 --> 00:26:20.339
nowadays people would look at that. And I even had PhD students after reading the paper, read that and be like, how is

00:26:20.339 --> 00:26:26.219
this groundbreaking? Everybody just does this. And I'm thinking, that's the point. That's the point. That paper was

00:26:26.219 --> 00:26:33.380
basically saying, everybody should be doing this. And in 2026, it's very easy to say, oh, but everyone's doing this,

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:41.979
whatever. So just the idea that those communities should be working together and integrating what they're doing on a

00:26:41.979 --> 00:26:49.380
foundational level, I think was a tectonic shift, intellectually speaking, for the Viz community and the database

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:56.140
community. And I think that's also why Tableau as a company had this like wild commercial success soon after that,

00:26:56.140 --> 00:27:04.859
because those communities just weren't connecting in a way that that company was able to kind of put them together at the

00:27:04.859 --> 00:27:11.420
time. So to be fair, there were definitely companies before and after who've done great things that are kind of in a similar

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:12.180
spirit, right?

00:27:12.619 --> 00:27:18.780
I didn't realize though, there was a research paper associated with Tableau and a lot of it. That's pretty crazy to hear.

