00:49:05.085 --> 00:49:07.644
Actually, it was from the tail end of my dissertation work.

00:49:08.644 --> 00:49:13.845
Basically you wanted to see, okay, well with recommendations, there could be

00:49:13.845 --> 00:49:17.325
this issue of performance where let's say you're analyzing a large dataset.

00:49:18.085 --> 00:49:21.884
What if the thing people look at is the thing you can recommend the fastest?

00:49:22.004 --> 00:49:23.445
Basically the thing they see first.

00:49:23.764 --> 00:49:30.644
How could that potentially introduce manipulation of people?

00:49:30.644 --> 00:49:33.685
Like what, what conclusions they might draw or what findings

00:49:33.685 --> 00:49:34.805
they might find in their data?

00:49:35.164 --> 00:49:38.925
So what we wanted to do was design an abstract experiment to try to test this.

00:49:39.524 --> 00:49:44.725
Could you, in a sense, manipulate what people found in a

00:49:44.725 --> 00:49:48.124
visualization purely through latency?

00:49:49.085 --> 00:49:52.884
Like how quickly or how slowly people might see the data

00:49:53.004 --> 00:49:54.085
or different parts of the data.

00:49:55.524 --> 00:49:59.164
So I designed this like really abstracted version of this experiment.

00:49:59.445 --> 00:50:03.044
I created this image collage and asked people to explore the collage of images.

00:50:03.044 --> 00:50:05.764
And then they were looking for certain, a certain target image.

00:50:06.565 --> 00:50:10.965
I actually put two targets, like two copies of that target in the collage.

00:50:12.124 --> 00:50:14.524
But the goal of the task was to only find one.

00:50:15.524 --> 00:50:17.884
And you can imagine kind of like Google Maps.

00:50:17.884 --> 00:50:21.884
Hey, you sometimes say you're on your phone and your data speed isn't that

00:50:21.884 --> 00:50:23.925
great and the map takes a while to load.

00:50:23.925 --> 00:50:26.205
Maybe it loads in kind of patchy.

00:50:26.524 --> 00:50:29.284
Imagine something kind of like that in this experiment, but it's an image

00:50:29.284 --> 00:50:32.244
collage so some images appear faster, slower than others.

00:50:33.364 --> 00:50:37.445
So I designed the latency for the images such that it would look kind of random.

00:50:38.404 --> 00:50:41.004
But in fact, it was definitely not random.

00:50:41.284 --> 00:50:45.325
I was manipulating how quickly certain images would appear.

00:50:47.044 --> 00:50:52.325
And I was trying to make it such that I only use latency to try to incentivize

00:50:52.325 --> 00:50:56.605
people to find one target over another in the collage.

00:50:57.565 --> 00:51:02.565
And the, the experiments showed that if the latency was pretty low overall,

00:51:02.565 --> 00:51:04.845
like relatively low, it didn't affect people as much.

00:51:05.485 --> 00:51:10.725
And then, but gradually as you ratcheted up the latency, like if you

00:51:10.725 --> 00:51:15.004
made it the tile, the images load in slow enough, you could definitely

00:51:15.004 --> 00:51:18.124
manipulate people to find a certain target in the collage.

00:51:18.124 --> 00:51:24.925
So it was just showing how latency matters, how you design a system matters,

00:51:24.925 --> 00:51:27.325
but also how you recommend things matters.

00:51:27.685 --> 00:51:32.044
If you do it purely based on performance and don't pay attention to what, you

00:51:32.044 --> 00:51:36.965
know, what you're prioritizing, like if you're, if you have options to show A or

00:51:36.965 --> 00:51:42.605
B and you always make A show up faster, depending on how much faster A shows up,

00:51:42.805 --> 00:51:46.605
you might be incentivizing the user to just always look at A and never look at B.

00:51:47.164 --> 00:51:52.485
That seems like super relevant in the era of LLMs again, where like latency

00:51:52.485 --> 00:51:54.364
is becoming so important again.

00:51:54.364 --> 00:51:55.485
Absolutely.

00:51:55.485 --> 00:51:59.685
So that to me was like a fun experiment that I enjoyed.

00:51:59.685 --> 00:52:01.404
I really enjoyed the design of the experiment.

00:52:01.404 --> 00:52:04.485
The takeaway was pretty straightforward in my opinion.

00:52:05.004 --> 00:52:08.565
I guess one thing that was surprising about it was people kind of thought of

00:52:08.565 --> 00:52:12.325
latency as a sort of binary thing, like, Oh, there's either too much or too little.

00:52:12.685 --> 00:52:15.845
But that experiment was showing it's actually kind of a gradual effect.

00:52:16.485 --> 00:52:20.164
And the more latency you have, the worse the effect becomes for

00:52:20.164 --> 00:52:22.124
people that get, become kind of biased.

00:52:22.685 --> 00:52:26.805
And the, I guess the latency between two things being relative as well, I

00:52:26.805 --> 00:52:29.085
guess, where two things are competing.

