So responsible data science is this giant umbrella.

00:53:22.485 --> 00:53:24.845
Lots of people work in this space.

00:53:25.244 --> 00:53:28.925
At a high level response, the whole point of data responsible data science is the

00:53:28.925 --> 00:53:35.205
idea that we want the outcomes of data science work to be responsible.

00:53:35.524 --> 00:53:39.725
We want that people are not discriminated against.

00:53:39.725 --> 00:53:45.925
We want that people are not harmed by the outputs of say models, large

00:53:45.925 --> 00:53:47.124
language models and otherwise.

00:53:47.845 --> 00:53:50.004
We want people to be treated fairly.

00:53:51.404 --> 00:53:51.685
Okay.

00:53:52.325 --> 00:53:55.845
So everybody who wants that and is working towards that, they fall under

00:53:55.845 --> 00:53:57.364
this responsibility science umbrella.

00:53:57.644 --> 00:54:01.685
What I think is really cool about the work that I'm doing, and this is in

00:54:01.685 --> 00:54:05.925
collaboration with a few professors who I want to shout out, so I want to shout

00:54:05.925 --> 00:54:12.284
out Emily Wall, kind of my longest standing collaborator on this work.

00:54:13.004 --> 00:54:17.805
Professor Amy Zhang, who Amy and I co-advised an awesome PhD student

00:54:17.805 --> 00:54:19.244
working in this space, Tiana Barrett.

00:54:19.764 --> 00:54:22.164
And then also Professor Yuichi Shoda.

00:54:22.485 --> 00:54:27.605
So Yuichi is a faculty member in psychology at UDAB and without Yuichi,

00:54:28.004 --> 00:54:30.485
we wouldn't be, I wouldn't be where I am now with these projects.

00:54:30.485 --> 00:54:33.925
So I think his perspective has been really helpful too.

00:54:34.445 --> 00:54:40.164
But the thing that always struck me about responsible data science work, where I

00:54:40.164 --> 00:54:45.004
think it's changing a bit more now, but years ago when Emily and I started

00:54:45.004 --> 00:54:49.685
talking about this problem, what struck us about it and what struck me about it

00:54:50.124 --> 00:54:56.004
especially is how it was always sort of treated as a algorithmic problem, a model

00:54:56.004 --> 00:54:58.085
training problem, or a data cleaning problem.

00:54:59.485 --> 00:55:02.524
The problem was always someone or something else.

00:55:02.565 --> 00:55:04.284
The problem was never us.

00:55:05.644 --> 00:55:07.244
And I was just thinking, wait a minute.

00:55:07.404 --> 00:55:09.685
So who does data science work?

00:55:10.164 --> 00:55:11.845
Is the data science worker the model?

00:55:11.845 --> 00:55:14.805
Is the data science worker the data or is it me and you?

00:55:15.404 --> 00:55:20.644
So if we're going to, you know, try to make data science work more responsible,

00:55:20.644 --> 00:55:24.845
should we be emphasizing the artifacts or should we be emphasizing

00:55:24.845 --> 00:55:26.085
the people doing the work?

00:55:27.124 --> 00:55:32.605
So what came out of that is this idea of behavior change interventions

00:55:32.805 --> 00:55:34.244
for responsible data science.

00:55:34.244 --> 00:55:38.805
So the idea that we should not just focus on whether or not a data set is

00:55:38.805 --> 00:55:41.404
responsible, a model's responsible.

00:55:41.404 --> 00:55:45.764
We should also be focusing on, you know, are the practices of the data

00:55:45.764 --> 00:55:52.085
scientists responsible and how does that contribute to ethical or unethical

00:55:52.085 --> 00:55:53.764
outcomes in data science?

00:55:54.124 --> 00:55:59.205
So that's the perspective that we brought to the community.

00:56:00.205 --> 00:56:03.565
Because initially people weren't really thinking about the problem in this way.

00:56:03.565 --> 00:56:05.325
They weren't talking about it in this way.

00:56:05.925 --> 00:56:08.124
People are actually kind of skeptical too about it.

00:56:08.124 --> 00:56:13.644
It took us a bit to actually publish our very first paper in that space where it

00:56:13.644 --> 00:56:18.445
was kind of a mixture of a literature review and vision paper describing what

00:56:18.445 --> 00:56:22.925
behavior change would mean in the context of responsible data science and what

00:56:22.925 --> 00:56:27.725
kinds of tools in psychology and in computer science that we could use to

00:56:27.725 --> 00:56:32.284
actually achieve that goal of trying to help people shift their behaviors.

00:56:33.284 --> 00:56:40.244
And then since then, we've had a couple, a few more papers on that, uh, line in

00:56:40.244 --> 00:56:46.004
that line of work where the most recent one is actually a paper at CHI, so top

00:56:46.004 --> 00:56:53.725
HCI conference, where we actually evaluated two existing behavior change

00:56:53.725 --> 00:56:59.965
intervention strategies in the context of a controlled, uh, data modeling task.

00:57:00.965 --> 00:57:06.004
So we asked people to train a machine learning model with some provided data

00:57:06.004 --> 00:57:07.605
that we gave them during the experiment.

00:57:08.404 --> 00:57:16.364
And then we, they experienced, uh, I think they experienced two

00:57:16.364 --> 00:57:17.845
different intervention strategies.

00:57:17.845 --> 00:57:20.644
So one was we primed them.

00:57:21.004 --> 00:57:26.965
So we had them read a little prompt that hopefully motivated them to be more

00:57:26.965 --> 00:57:29.524
responsible in their actions and training model.

00:57:30.565 --> 00:57:35.124
And then a separate intervention where we actually just loaded, um, like a mo- a

00:57:35.124 --> 00:57:38.925
modular plugin into the notebook they were using to train the model.

00:57:39.524 --> 00:57:44.244
And they could use that tool, that specific tool to check the fairness

00:57:44.244 --> 00:57:45.445
of their model and improve it.

00:57:46.605 --> 00:57:47.044
Yeah.

00:57:47.085 --> 00:57:47.244
Yeah.

00:57:47.244 --> 00:57:48.445
So sorry, I have it here.

00:57:48.445 --> 00:57:53.284
I was trying to grab the, but this is, um, evaluating behavior change

00:57:53.284 --> 00:57:55.524
interventions for responsible data science.

00:57:55.524 --> 00:57:55.965
Is that?

00:57:56.085 --> 00:57:56.605
Yes.

00:57:56.685 --> 00:57:56.884
Yeah.

00:57:56.884 --> 00:57:57.284
Yeah.

00:57:57.284 --> 00:57:57.364
Nice.

00:57:57.364 --> 00:57:59.884
Evaluating behavior change interventions for responsible data science.

00:58:00.605 --> 00:58:00.884
Yeah.

00:58:01.164 --> 00:58:06.805
So a cool outcome of that work was that priming people to think

00:58:06.805 --> 00:58:09.445
responsibly did shift their behaviors.

00:58:09.965 --> 00:58:13.644
The, they did have an increase in the range of responsible

00:58:13.644 --> 00:58:16.284
activities they did during the session.

00:58:17.764 --> 00:58:21.845
But the fairness of their models did not actually.

00:58:22.403 --> 00:58:30.443
But the fairness of their models did not actually get better from a significance perspective.

00:58:30.443 --> 00:58:34.803
We did not notice a significant improvement in the fairness of the models.

00:58:34.803 --> 00:58:39.883
So just motivating people to do better, basically just telling them to do better, and them feeling

00:58:39.883 --> 00:58:43.183
like they want to do better doesn't actually lead to anything getting better.

00:58:43.183 --> 00:58:47.603
So things change, but not necessarily in a measurably better way.

00:58:47.603 --> 00:58:51.282
So giving them tools to actually show them.

00:58:39.542 --> 00:58:46.102
So things change but not necessarily in a measurably better way. So giving them tools

00:58:46.102 --> 00:58:50.582
to actually show them like this is what better looks like in terms of outcomes

00:58:51.222 --> 00:58:53.862
and pairing that with the motivation, that actually works.

00:58:54.502 --> 00:59:01.142
I think that behavior change framing is like so interesting and like powerful. Like it's not

00:59:01.142 --> 00:59:07.462
something, I don't know, like I think in you know like computer science and statistics it's so easy

00:59:07.462 --> 00:59:13.862
to get locked in on like the algorithms and the mathematics and forget like oh actually it's

00:59:13.862 --> 00:59:19.862
humans doing all this and we have these levers to pull on how the humans use our tools and that's

00:59:19.862 --> 00:59:23.302
just as valuable. Just as powerful.

00:59:23.302 --> 00:59:25.621
Yeah. Yeah.

00:59:25.621 --> 00:59:28.182
What was the tool called? Is it Equitas?

00:59:28.182 --> 00:59:30.662
Oh, Equitas. Equitas. Yeah, okay.

00:59:31.621 --> 00:59:34.741
Yeah, what does that look like? It runs inside the notebook, is that?

00:59:34.741 --> 00:59:40.421
It does run inside the notebook. It shows you some bias metrics and you can pick specific

00:59:40.982 --> 00:59:43.941
subgroups in your dataset that you want to compare against. For example,

00:59:44.581 --> 00:59:50.102
a dataset that we asked participants to train models with was sort of a classic

00:59:51.702 --> 00:59:57.941
like loan application datasets. So a demographic group you could compare would be like men versus

00:59:57.941 --> 01:00:04.102
women and their loan applications. So you could see if your model would be say biased against

01:00:04.102 --> 01:00:08.422
women and be more likely to reject their loan applications.

01:00:10.181 --> 01:00:15.382
So it's like versus the prime, which is more of a nudge. It gives them some like

01:00:15.382 --> 01:00:18.982
tools to kind of implement some like checks or...

01:00:18.982 --> 01:00:25.702
It's more about framing. So the prime is really just a prompt that encourages you to think like

01:00:25.702 --> 01:00:30.821
you could think about saying, hey, with the loan, the credit loan dataset,

01:00:30.982 --> 01:00:36.022
it's possible that women are more likely discriminated against than men and more

01:00:36.022 --> 01:00:40.422
likely to have their loans declined. So you could say something like that and like,

01:00:40.422 --> 01:00:44.022
oh, please keep this in mind when you're training your models.

01:00:44.581 --> 01:00:49.062
And as I'm understanding, it's like people will... The prime actually does nudge people

01:00:49.062 --> 01:00:52.982
in that they kind of, they've both like self-report. They're like, oh, I did

01:00:53.702 --> 01:00:56.582
follow these kinds of like principles more.

01:00:57.461 --> 01:01:02.021
Yeah. People would say, oh yeah, I was definitely more motivated, like being reminded of that or,

01:01:02.982 --> 01:01:07.222
oh, as a woman, I can resonate, that resonates with me and I would not want

01:01:07.222 --> 01:01:10.661
people to be discriminated against in that way. It definitely motivated people.

01:01:10.661 --> 01:01:15.142
And we did notice a behavioral shift. But I think the important thing is just

01:01:15.862 --> 01:01:19.142
inducing a change in behavior does not guarantee the outcomes change.

01:01:20.181 --> 01:01:23.541
You have to induce the behavior in a deliberate direction.

01:01:23.702 --> 01:01:29.862
Yeah, gotcha. Like, I guess, like provide the skill or capability to kind of

01:01:31.222 --> 01:01:33.222
actually produce a fair model.

01:01:34.821 --> 01:01:38.582
Yeah. Giving them the capabilities to measure the fairness of their model.

01:01:38.582 --> 01:01:44.982
And then also to fix the model when it's not at the level that they want or if they want it to be

01:01:44.982 --> 01:01:45.482
more fair.

01:01:46.261 --> 01:01:51.862
Yeah. Gotcha. Yeah. It's super interesting. And as I understand it too, the, like,

01:01:51.862 --> 01:01:59.621
the evaluation of, like, there's two parts, as I understand it, like the responsible data science,

01:01:59.621 --> 01:02:03.862
you have the framework for it. But this paper is really interesting that it's also attempting

01:02:03.862 --> 01:02:10.582
to evaluate it and provide like a behavioral experiment. And I think, as I recall, I think

01:02:10.582 --> 01:02:18.821
the paper says it's like one of the first controlled experiments in kind of like evaluating.

01:02:19.782 --> 01:02:22.582
Yeah. I'd be curious to hear a little bit more about that.

01:02:24.021 --> 01:02:29.302
Yeah. So behavior change, behavior change for responsible data science is just,

01:02:29.302 --> 01:02:35.222
in my opinion, understudied. There should be way more people than the, me and the faculty

01:02:35.222 --> 01:02:40.661
I mentioned studying this problem. And there are some people studying this problem. So I'll

01:02:40.661 --> 01:02:45.942
acknowledge, like, this is not an empty bucket, but there should be way more people studying it.

01:02:45.942 --> 01:02:50.101
And I think part of the challenge in studying this problem is that we are not necessarily

01:02:50.101 --> 01:02:54.582
well-equipped with the tools that we need to tackle these kinds of behavioral problems,

01:02:54.582 --> 01:02:58.422
but there are other communities that have studied it for a long time, like psychology.

01:02:59.541 --> 01:03:06.261
Behavior change is a really important aspect of psychology research. And so what we are trying

01:03:06.261 --> 01:03:14.422
to introduce through these papers are strategies and tools that we could adopt where we maybe,

01:03:14.422 --> 01:03:19.142
we could even collaborate with researchers in psychology, like UHE's collaboration with us

01:03:19.142 --> 01:03:26.022
is an example of that, where we don't have to come up with all the answers as computer scientists.

01:03:26.022 --> 01:03:29.862
And in fact, we shouldn't, because a lot of us are not behavioral scientists. A lot of us are not

01:03:30.582 --> 01:03:34.982
psychology researchers. We should be collaborating with other people and we should be learning from

01:03:34.982 --> 01:03:41.781
them to develop solutions. So all of this work is really trying to build infrastructure intellectually

01:03:41.781 --> 01:03:46.022
and, you know, mechanically speaking, like with designing experiments to measure success,

01:03:46.902 --> 01:03:52.582
to see, you know, what could behavior change interventions look like in this context if we

01:03:52.582 --> 01:03:56.742
were to build more of them and, you know, what are the right things to do and what are the kind

01:03:56.742 --> 01:04:02.742
of the pitfalls we should avoid in those designs. I saw a lot of interesting measures like cognitive

01:04:02.742 --> 01:04:08.501
load and things like that. So it's cool to see kind of the full array of like instrumentation,

01:04:09.381 --> 01:04:11.702
you know, in this experiment.

01:04:12.501 --> 01:04:17.622
Yeah. I'm glad you mentioned cognitive load too, because an important thing to acknowledge in that

01:04:17.622 --> 01:04:24.582
work is that we're asking people to literally do more work than they would normally do when you're

01:04:24.582 --> 01:04:30.742
asking them to do data science work responsibly, which also means introducing a cognitive burden

01:04:31.381 --> 01:04:36.101
on top of what they already have to deal with as a user of data science tools.

01:04:37.061 --> 01:04:44.902
And the load measures from that study show that, reflect that. It's not shocking, right? It makes

01:04:44.902 --> 01:04:49.302
sense that if you're asking people to not just worry about model performance, but also whether

01:04:49.302 --> 01:04:55.302
it's discriminating against people, that's more work that they had to do. But it was cool that

01:04:55.302 --> 01:04:59.462
those measures in the paper show that. Yeah, it's a really cool approach.

01:05:00.182 --> 01:05:08.821
And then I know the other one I saw that you put out recently was, wait, what's this?

01:05:10.261 --> 01:05:15.462
The African Data Ethics, a Discursive Framework for Black Decolonial AI.

01:05:17.142 --> 01:05:20.661
Yeah. I'm really curious to hear more about that paper as well.

01:05:21.702 --> 01:05:26.422
Yeah. So I definitely want to acknowledge the intellectual leader of that work was Tiana

01:05:26.422 --> 01:05:33.221
Barrett. So she's a PhD student here at UW, co-advised by me and Amy Zhang. That was kind of

01:05:35.221 --> 01:05:41.142
her first major project as a PhD student here at UW. And I felt like she did a fantastic job with

01:05:41.142 --> 01:05:47.702
it. And so what's interesting to me about that work is this idea that, so, you know, in responsible

01:05:47.702 --> 01:05:52.101
data science, we care a lot about what it means to do something right and what it means to do

01:05:52.101 --> 01:05:59.381
something wrong, ethically speaking. And a lot of how various communities try to write that down

01:05:59.381 --> 01:06:06.582
are through frameworks, ethics frameworks. What we noticed is that in these ethics frameworks,

01:06:06.582 --> 01:06:11.941
you have them for a lot of different communities, but it seemed like African countries were being

01:06:11.941 --> 01:06:16.982
left out of that conversation of what, like, what is right and what is wrong in the context of

01:06:16.982 --> 01:06:24.982
interacting with, engaging with African peoples. And it seemed like we could develop a framework

01:06:25.781 --> 01:06:34.341
to reflect the values and the ethics of different African countries to fill that gap. So that was

01:06:34.341 --> 01:06:42.182
the idea behind that work. And Tiana worked really hard to take into account African,

01:06:42.742 --> 01:06:47.541
like Sub-Saharan African philosophy, ethics work,

01:06:50.821 --> 01:06:57.381
kind of thought leaders in terms of how AI should be incorporated into their cultures,

01:06:57.941 --> 01:07:03.781
things like that, and kind of putting it all together to review, okay, if we were to create

01:07:03.781 --> 01:07:12.101
one framework to exemplify these principles that should be upheld, what would that look like?

01:07:12.661 --> 01:07:18.422
And what gaps are we filling compared to other ethics frameworks? Like what, which of these

01:07:18.422 --> 01:07:22.901
principles might be covered in current frameworks and which are not? And I would say anyone who's

01:07:22.901 --> 01:07:28.742
really excited about it, please reach out to Tiana. She loves talking about her work and that paper.

01:07:29.781 --> 01:07:34.901
And a lot of people have been really excited about it. So I just encourage folks to reach out.

01:07:35.701 --> 01:07:41.541
Do you know what y'all might do next as next steps building on the paper?

01:07:42.262 --> 01:07:49.062
Yeah, that's a great question. So one thing that really bothers me about ethics frameworks is that

01:07:49.062 --> 01:07:58.422
they're so hard to use in a practical sense. I could go out, read a bunch of books, learn all

01:07:58.422 --> 01:08:03.302
there is to know about these frameworks, but then how do I actually apply it to my data science work?

01:08:04.182 --> 01:08:09.541
And then also a more recent project that Tiana has been working on is this idea of, well,

01:08:10.341 --> 01:08:17.062
how exactly do data scientists operationalize their ethics and their values in their own work?

01:08:17.862 --> 01:08:22.742
And the answer is it's really dependent on the environment that they're in and the opportunities

01:08:22.742 --> 01:08:27.701
that they're afforded to express those values through their work. And some people haven't

01:08:27.701 --> 01:08:31.461
thought as deeply about it as others, but even people who think deeply about it, depending on

01:08:31.942 --> 01:08:37.382
where you work, you might need like outside projects to be able to do that, to really

01:08:38.661 --> 01:08:44.901
closely align your values with the practices in data science that you follow. Because your company

01:08:44.901 --> 01:08:52.182
might be hostile towards it. They might not care. Like you better meet your key performance metrics

01:08:52.901 --> 01:08:57.701
and I'm not going to give you any extra resources for this other stuff where it might be that

01:08:57.701 --> 01:09:03.062
resources get cut back at work. So then the time and the energy and resources you had to do

01:09:03.062 --> 01:09:08.742
some more of the responsible aspects might be reduced. So it's very situational

01:09:10.182 --> 01:09:13.141
and there are a lot of factors involved. So what we're interested in is, well,

01:09:13.781 --> 01:09:18.742
for the question of if it's hard to figure out what your ethics are, what values you want to

01:09:18.742 --> 01:09:22.742
uphold through your data science work, could frameworks help you and could we make frameworks

01:09:22.742 --> 01:09:30.182
easier to use in that direction? Another question is if there are these barriers,

01:09:30.182 --> 01:09:34.822
these environmental barriers to helping you, you know, practice data science more responsibly in

01:09:34.822 --> 01:09:40.581
the way that you want, from a technology perspective, what can we and what can we not

01:09:40.581 --> 01:09:44.661
do to support that? You're saying outside projects, they might need outside projects

01:09:44.661 --> 01:09:49.382
because they just don't have the resources. Yeah. Or the bandwidth or the company's just

01:09:49.382 --> 01:09:55.862
not interested or it doesn't want them to do that work within the context of the company.

01:09:55.862 --> 01:10:00.901
So then maybe they may have a passion project outside where they're sort of,

01:10:01.541 --> 01:10:06.581
I don't want to say atoning, but like allowing themselves to, to focus on where their heart is.

01:10:07.302 --> 01:10:12.502
Yeah. It's interesting to think about too, the, in terms of responsibility that I guess,

01:10:12.502 --> 01:10:17.221
if you're a data scientist within a company, the question of like, what is my responsibility?

01:10:17.221 --> 01:10:21.862
And if I don't have resources in the company to kind of like meet that responsibility,

01:10:22.822 --> 01:10:30.982
should I maybe do, do I need to put in work to kind of like build the skills to,

01:10:31.942 --> 01:10:40.021
to behave responsibly? Or even just how hard should I fight to try to make these resources

01:10:40.021 --> 01:10:46.502
available so that people can perform their work responsibly in the way that aligns with my values?

01:10:47.221 --> 01:10:50.822
Cause the organization has values and then the individual has values and there's this

01:10:50.822 --> 01:10:53.701
question of how they intersect. Yeah. That's a great point.

01:10:55.062 --> 01:11:02.021
Heather, have you run into much discussion around or thought about responsible data science?

01:11:02.742 --> 01:11:09.781
Yeah. I think we, we think a lot about, like I don't, like we don't use like responsible,

01:11:10.422 --> 01:11:16.982
but we think about a lot of these similar things in particular, like, you know, for me,

01:11:16.982 --> 01:11:23.302
I think a lot about the, like the pit of success and like, how do you kind of guide people into

01:11:23.302 --> 01:11:27.701
doing like the right thing or the better, like making the right thing or the better thing,

01:11:27.701 --> 01:11:33.221
like easier than the hard thing, easier than the bad thing or the worst thing.

01:11:34.422 --> 01:11:39.541
And I think it's fascinating to like, we now have a lot more levels to pull in terms of like

01:11:39.541 --> 01:11:45.221
prompting LLNs and giving them tools and having, you know, different agents review your work

01:11:45.221 --> 01:11:51.701
afterwards with different, with different lenses. So I think it's like, it's an exciting time to be

01:11:51.701 --> 01:11:57.141
thinking about how can we make these frameworks more actionable for, for, you know, real data

01:11:57.141 --> 01:12:02.502
scientists who of course want to do their best, but have so many other pressures on their time.

01:12:02.502 --> 01:12:08.182
Yeah, absolutely. I was going to say one direction I'm really interested in moving in the future is

01:12:08.182 --> 01:12:13.701
this idea of, yeah, it might not necessarily be right versus wrong in this kind of more nuanced

01:12:13.701 --> 01:12:19.942
space. Something I think we're running into and that we see is that inaction might be the kind of

01:12:19.942 --> 01:12:24.182
the biggest problem, inaction along different dimensions, because it's pretty clear, right?

01:12:24.182 --> 01:12:31.141
That if data science, data scientists and engineers and so on do what they believe is rational or what

01:12:31.141 --> 01:12:37.942
they believe is like reasonable and are not actively doing actions where they're like,

01:12:38.742 --> 01:12:44.661
you know, I should be doing additional things to make this as responsible as possible versus,

01:12:44.661 --> 01:12:50.742
oh, I don't think I'm doing harm. So I'm, I think I'm fine. We still end up in all sorts of bad

01:12:50.742 --> 01:12:56.341
situations. So it's not that people aren't rational or people aren't reasonable. It's that the rational

01:12:56.341 --> 01:13:02.581
and reasonable, like rational behavior is not in the, in the current context, the definition of

01:13:02.901 --> 01:13:12.581
rationality might not be ethical. So if you define rational as responsible, you end up in a different

01:13:12.581 --> 01:13:17.862
frame, which might lead a totally separate line of decision-making that we come very different from

01:13:17.862 --> 01:13:22.581
what we do right now. So something I'm, Emily Wall and I are interested in following is this

01:13:22.581 --> 01:13:29.382
idea of rationality in data science and where it might get us into trouble and how other frames,

01:13:29.382 --> 01:13:33.141
like other definitions of rationality might help us kind of break out of that cycle.

01:13:33.701 --> 01:13:39.461
Wanting to make money is rational, for example, but can lead us all sorts of directions.

01:13:40.021 --> 01:13:41.141
Sticky situations.

01:13:42.101 --> 01:13:46.901
Yeah. And it's not necessarily bad, right? But sometimes the outcomes are bad. So the question

01:13:46.901 --> 01:13:51.382
is like, how does that frame, that definition of rational get us in trouble?

01:13:52.021 --> 01:13:58.341
Do you have advice for people maybe starting out in data science today or recommendations for them?

01:13:58.822 --> 01:14:00.822
You mean for like responsible data science?

01:14:02.262 --> 01:14:04.101
Yeah. For responsible data science.

01:14:04.742 --> 01:14:11.622
What I would, what I would say is you don't have to like read a bajillion books on it or anything,

01:14:11.622 --> 01:14:16.581
maybe pick one book, like Data Feminism and check it out. And you don't have to necessarily

01:14:16.581 --> 01:14:22.822
commit yourself to doing everything in that book, but just kind of, I think it's useful to know

01:14:22.822 --> 01:14:27.781
what's out there and then you yourself can decide how you want to incorporate it. And then I would

01:14:27.781 --> 01:14:37.221
say for each tool or feature that you're going to use to automate your work, see if there's another

01:14:37.221 --> 01:14:44.341
tool or feature that you can use to evaluate it from a responsible perspective. Like if you're

01:14:44.341 --> 01:14:48.422
going to train models, see if there's already tools out there that could help you check how

01:14:48.422 --> 01:14:53.302
fair they are. If you're data cleaning and you're going to do some automation, see if there's

01:14:53.302 --> 01:14:56.742
anything that can help check for like bias.

01:14:57.942 --> 01:15:02.982
It seems like such a helpful rule of thumb, like for each thing you use, find a thing to evaluate

01:15:02.982 --> 01:15:12.502
it as like such a practical piece of advice to kind of like try to counterbalance. Like an easy

01:15:12.502 --> 01:15:19.302
thing to kind of like nudge a person. I feel like that, that strikes me as really helpful for being

01:15:19.302 --> 01:15:26.101
able to kind of like put this into an operation. Yeah. If I was starting at the very beginning,

01:15:26.101 --> 01:15:31.701
that's what I would do. I would think about, you know, almost like unit testing. If I already do

01:15:31.701 --> 01:15:38.182
the equivalent of that for any tool I want to add to my toolbox, you know, what checks and balances

01:15:38.182 --> 01:15:44.181
can I just automatically put in place as I add to the toolbox. Hadley and Wes, any other questions?

